###############################################################################
# Scenario: idempotency + double-close protection (the concurrency safeguards)
#
#  1. Idempotent OPEN  - sending the same client_request_id twice creates only
#     ONE trade (enforced by the UNIQUE client_request_id constraint). The second
#     request is accepted (202) and gets its own trade_id, but the worker writes
#     no row - so that id has no valuation (404).
#
#  2. Double-close     - closing the same trade twice: only the first succeeds
#     (guarded UPDATE ... WHERE status='ACTIVE'); the second is a no-op, so the
#     finalized realized PnL does NOT change.
#
# Needs the full stack. Send top-to-bottom; wait ~3s where noted.
###############################################################################

@marketData = http://localhost:8001
@books      = http://localhost:8004
@actions    = http://localhost:8008
@pricing    = http://localhost:8002

### Book
# @name createBook
POST {{books}}/books
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "IDEMPOTENCY_{{$timestamp}}", "expected_asset_class": "EQUITY" }

###
@bookId = {{createBook.response.body.$.book_id}}

### Live market to fill the open
# @name snap
GET {{marketData}}/snapshot

###
@ask = {{snap.response.body.$.spots.ACME.ask}}
@bid = {{snap.response.body.$.spots.ACME.bid}}

# ============================ 1. IDEMPOTENT OPEN ============================

### First OPEN (captures the client_request_id + trade_id T1)
# @name openOnce
POST {{actions}}/trade-actions
Content-Type: application/json

{ "action_type": "OPEN_TRADE", "client_request_id": "req-idem-{{$timestamp}}", "book_id": "{{bookId}}", "asset_class": "EQUITY", "symbol": "ACME", "side": "BUY", "quantity": 100, "trade_price": {{ask}} }

###
@crid = {{openOnce.response.body.$.client_request_id}}
@t1 = {{openOnce.response.body.$.trade_id}}

### Re-send the SAME client_request_id (mints id T2, but the worker dedups -> no row)
# @name openDup
POST {{actions}}/trade-actions
Content-Type: application/json

{ "action_type": "OPEN_TRADE", "client_request_id": "{{crid}}", "book_id": "{{bookId}}", "asset_class": "EQUITY", "symbol": "ACME", "side": "BUY", "quantity": 100, "trade_price": {{ask}} }

###
@t2 = {{openDup.response.body.$.trade_id}}

### --- wait ~3s --- T1 was created and is priced
GET {{pricing}}/valuations/{{t1}}

### T2 (the duplicate) was NOT created -> 404 (idempotency held: one crid = one trade)
GET {{pricing}}/valuations/{{t2}}

### Trade-action counters: `duplicates` ticked up (the deduped open)
### (counters are cumulative across the service's lifetime)
GET {{actions}}/queue/status

# ============================ 2. DOUBLE-CLOSE ==============================

### Close T1 at the live bid
POST {{actions}}/trade-actions
Content-Type: application/json

{ "action_type": "CLOSE_TRADE", "client_request_id": "close-{{$timestamp}}", "trade_id": "{{t1}}", "close_price": {{bid}}, "close_reason": "RANDOM_TRADE_OUT" }

### --- wait ~3s --- realized PnL finalized (unrealized=0)
GET {{pricing}}/valuations/{{t1}}

### Close T1 AGAIN at an absurd price -> guarded no-op (already CLOSED)
POST {{actions}}/trade-actions
Content-Type: application/json

{ "action_type": "CLOSE_TRADE", "trade_id": "{{t1}}", "close_price": 1 }

### --- wait ~3s --- realized is UNCHANGED (the second close had no effect)
GET {{pricing}}/valuations/{{t1}}

### Trade-action counters: `closed` ticked up once, `rejected` for the double-close
GET {{actions}}/queue/status